# contains the code for sema (right now stubs)

DONT FORGET CLASS LIFECYCLE PASS


Notes:
- Missing an important one on figuring out what an expr is (lvalue or rvalue or xvalue)
- Also the import passes to fold imports and thier uses in a file to the right thing like:
```kairo
import std::io;
import std::mem::alloc;

fn main() {
    io::print("Hello, World!");
    var ptr = alloc(1024); // alloc is a function from std::mem
}
```
after transformation, the code should be:
```kairo
module io {
    fn print(msg: str);
}

module mem {
    fn alloc(size: usize) -> *u8;
}

fb main() {
    io::print("Hello, World!");
    var ptr = mem::alloc(1024);
}
```
this is roughly what the pass does internally, this is the abs first pass done, since this unlocks the rest of sema to be fully independent on a per TU basis allowing for parallel processing of translation units - atleast untill we get to mono pass.

Mono pass is split into 2 parts, part 1 fully parrallel pulls all the invokes of a genericed thing fn class strcut wtv, so foo<i32>() is a usage of the generic function.

pass part 2 is in sync, it goes to wehre the def for each generic is found and adds a new instantiation for each usage, while externing the specific instantiation where its used.

Mono pass is a crucial part of the compilation process that handles the instantiation of generic functions and types.